1
00:00:31,950 --> 00:00:37,150
This is Walnut DataBridge, built with Tencent CodeBuddy and wired for Tencent's Hunyuan.

2
00:00:37,150 --> 00:00:39,550
Let's start with Aspire's own question.

3
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,200
Rachel is the money-laundering reporting officer at a fintech.

4
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,600
She asks Aspire's question: why has Velquarry Trading's account been restricted?

5
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:54,200
One answer, and every sentence points at a passage she can open right now.

6
00:00:54,200 --> 00:01:01,000
Click one, and you see the exact lines, the version, and when it was checked: seconds ago.

7
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,200
Each source is read through its own connector, with her permissions checked live.

8
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,600
And now the screens you ask from work in Chinese too.

9
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:23,450
Here is a map of what Jordan, a backend engineer, can open, built from the same permission checks.

10
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:31,050
Compare two people, and Ken, an outside contractor, has a small corner of the company, with no greyed-out boxes.

11
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:33,850
For Ken, the rest simply isn't there.

12
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:41,500
Now the same question from two people: Amy, an account manager, left; Rachel, right.

13
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:44,300
And what should I tell the customer?

14
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:49,100
Amy gets the approved customer script, with its sources, and nothing else.

15
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:53,500
Rachel gets the actual case: the flagged transfers, and the review.

16
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:55,500
Same system, two different truths.

17
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:01,100
In Singapore, telling Amy that a suspicious-transaction report even exists is a criminal offence.

18
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,100
So the system never does.

19
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:13,550
Now Ken on his own.

20
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:16,350
On the left, he asks about Velquarry.

21
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:19,950
On the right, about a company that doesn't exist.

22
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:22,750
The answers are identical, word for word.

23
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:25,550
A restricted file looks exactly like nothing.

24
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:29,150
We ran four hundred pairs like this: zero differences.

25
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,800
And now Amy's answer can become the reply itself.

26
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:44,200
She asks for a draft: the approved wording, built from the cited facts, and never sent.

27
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:49,400
Jordan's answer offers a ticket, but it's only a proposal until he confirms.

28
00:02:51,850 --> 00:02:59,850
Answers can also become files: a table whose figures point at their sources, and a chart from the same facts.

29
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,900
Now the compliance side, as Maya.

30
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:06,700
This is Rachel's request, end to end.

31
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:13,900
Each bar is a real timing: the index filter and the live source checks first, then the answer.

32
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:18,700
These three records were sealed and chained before Rachel saw a word.

33
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:23,900
And below, every document that was considered, with its verdict and the reason.

34
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:25,700
Maya sees every decision.

35
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:28,100
She never sees the answer itself.

36
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:38,350
Maya can also ask the audit log in plain English: everything Rachel accessed in the last seven days.

37
00:03:38,350 --> 00:03:41,550
She sees the filter first, then the rows.

38
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:44,750
Verify re-checks every link in the chain: intact.

39
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:46,550
Now the fun part.

40
00:03:46,550 --> 00:03:50,550
The tamper demo copies the log and attacks the copy.

41
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:56,950
It changes one old decision, from "forbidden" to "verified", and Verify points at the exact entry.

42
00:03:56,950 --> 00:04:00,150
Deleting or rolling back records is caught too.

43
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:02,550
The real log was never touched.

44
00:04:02,550 --> 00:04:06,150
That's what we mean by answers you can prove.

45
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:17,200
Maya's analytics come straight from the verified log: how many questions, how many answered, how fast.

46
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:21,200
Seeded history is labelled, and one click leaves it out.

47
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:26,850
The Trust centre lists the seven controls behind all of this.

48
00:04:26,850 --> 00:04:34,450
Each one is enforced in code, mapped to Singapore's AI and data rules, and proved by a named test.

49
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:40,700
It plugs into Tencent WorkBuddy, so people can ask from where they already work.

50
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:47,100
It's an MCP agent: each connector carries one person's signed token, so Jordan's gets Jordan's answer.

51
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:59,000
We built the whole platform: connectors to every source, AI agents that build new adapters, a federated engine across systems, and an evidence graph.

52
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:05,000
The Internal Brain runs on its core: the grounding wall and the sealed audit trail.

53
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Inside DataBridge, Jordan can look at the engine's own graph.

54
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,200
It's shown here; it isn't used to answer.

55
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:20,300
And it's wired for Tencent Hunyuan through TokenHub, packaged to deploy on Tencent Cloud.

56
00:05:20,300 --> 00:05:26,700
Until Hy3 is switched on, answers are AI-generated from your cited sources, through the same checks.

57
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,200
We didn't just demo it, we attacked it.

58
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:36,200
Seven thousand three hundred and seventy-four adversarial requests: zero leaks.

59
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:38,600
Four hundred paired questions: zero differences.

60
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:45,800
And one thousand two hundred and seventy-eight backend tests, plus one hundred and eighty-five browser tests, none failing.

61
00:05:48,650 --> 00:05:50,450
What's it worth?

62
00:05:50,450 --> 00:05:59,250
For a six-hundred-person fintech, if each person saves ninety minutes a week, that's about one point three million US dollars a year.

63
00:05:59,250 --> 00:06:01,650
It's an estimate, not a measurement.

64
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:06,050
And it pays for itself at about seven minutes a week.

65
00:06:09,300 --> 00:06:15,700
We built it with Tencent CodeBuddy: nine recorded sessions, including security reviews that found real bugs.

66
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:22,550
Every company's AI will be asked to prove itself.

67
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:24,350
Walnut DataBridge.

68
00:06:24,350 --> 00:06:26,150
Answers you can prove.

69
00:06:26,150 --> 00:06:29,750
We'd love one pilot team at one regulated company.

70
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:31,250
Thanks for watching!
